## State Summary

**Scope harvested:** OTLP ingress (nginx) sender-side configuration for TCP 4317 (gRPC) and TCP 4318 (HTTP) only, per task scope. Self-provisioned the `observability-readonly` read-only service from the descriptor fetched via `browser-automation-service` (serviceId `cmupau37e00xiyxnr7se8aa28`, endpoint `http://127.0.0.1:3114/mcp`). Two read-only calls made: `stack_health`, `get_ingress_config`. No writes, reloads, or lifecycle calls issued — read-only surface only, as required. Teardown (`registry.delete`) deferred to SYNTHESIZE per protocol — service registration remains active until then.

**Stack liveness** (`stack_health`): `prometheus: up`, `grafana: up`, `otel_collector: up`. The declared OTLP ingress itself is present and responding — **target-absent is ruled out.**

### Witnessed rendering — `otlp-ingress.conf` (as-deployed file mount, verbatim)

```
# OTLP ingress allowlist — the lab's enforcement point for "which senders may export telemetry".
# Fronts BOTH OTLP receivers: gRPC (4317) and HTTP (4318). The collector accepts OTLP only on a
# private network shared with this proxy, so these two server blocks are the only way in.
# nginx evaluates allow/deny IN ORDER, first match wins. Read-only to the harvest service
# (obs-mcp get_ingress_config); changed only by an operator applying an approved change package,
# then `docker exec obs-ingress nginx -s reload`.
#
# BASELINE (brownfield): no sender restriction yet — `allow all` on both. Authorising a specific
# sender set means inserting its allow lines ABOVE `allow all` and replacing `allow all` with
# `deny all`, in BOTH server blocks.

# OTLP/gRPC
server {
    listen 4317;
    http2 on;

    location / {
        allow all;

        grpc_pass grpc://172.31.114.10:4317;
    }
}

# OTLP/http
server {
    listen 4318;
    client_max_body_size 16m;          # OTLP batches; nginx's 1m default would 413 real exporters

    location / {
        allow all;

        proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_actr;
    }
}
```
*(source: `get_ingress_config`, field `files["otlp-ingress.conf"]`, labeled by the service as `"as-deployed files (ro mount)"`, `"fronts": "otel-collector OTLP gRPC :4317 and OTLP/http :4318"`)*

### Witnessed rendering — `operator-access.conf` (unrelated file, quoted for completeness, NOT part of the sender allowlist)

```
# OPERATOR injection path — rig plumbing, NOT part of the sender allowlist (otlp-ingress.conf).
# Lets the lab operator inject demo telemetry with curl regardless of how the sender allowlist is
# set, so an approved restriction never locks the demo out. Reachable only from the rig HOST, which
# arrives from the pinned default network's gateway (docker-compose.yml `networks.default`).
# A change authorising OTLP senders has no reason to touch this file.

server {
    listen 14318;
    client_max_body_size 16m;

    location / {
        allow 172.20.0.1;
        deny all;

        proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
    }
}
```
This listens on a distinct port (14318), outside the declared scope (4317/4318); reported only as a boundary fact — the Architect must not touch it.

## Branch determination

| Condition | Applies? | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
| `observability-config.target-absent` | **No** | `stack_health` reports otel_collector `up`; `get_ingress_config` returned both server blocks for 4317 and 4318 — ingress exists. |
| `observability-config.target-empty / enforcer-absent` | **No** | Both ports carry an explicit `location / { allow all; ... }` stanza — this is configuration, not "no configuration at all". |
| **`observability-config.existing-grant`** | **Yes — primary branch** | Both server blocks (4317, 4318) contain an explicit `allow all;` directive, evaluated first-match-wins by nginx. The file's own comment confirms: *"BASELINE (brownfield): no sender restriction yet — `allow all` on both."* This is existing sender configuration (an unrestricted grant), so the architecture must **replace** it: insert the exporter-range CIDR's `allow` lines above `allow all` and flip `allow all` → `deny all` in BOTH server blocks, per the program's `observabilityConfig.existingGrantPolicy`. |
| `observability-config.principal-unseen` | **Yes — orthogonal flag, must also be carried forward** | The read-only tool surface (`get_ingress_config`, `stack_health`) exposes only the ingress's own config files — it returns no evidence of what sits between the exporter and this nginx at the network layer (no docker-network/NAT/LB inspection tool exists on this surface). Neither server block configures `set_real_ip_from`/`real_ip_header`/`proxy_protocol`, so nginx's `allow`/`deny` evaluates `$remote_addr` — the directly-connecting peer's address — by default. Whether that peer address is the exporter's **true, unmodified** source address at the point nginx evaluates it cannot be confirmed from what was harvested (no routing/NAT topology diagram or tool output was returned). This is a genuine harvest gap, not an inferred fault. |

**Net:** the dominant structural branch is **existing-grant** (explicit `allow all`, both ports) — the Architect designs a **replace** (not create) against this witnessed baseline. **Independently**, `principal-unseen` also applies as a path-integrity gap the harvest could not close; per the program's branch table, the downstream package must still author the change and **record the source-visibility premise as UNTESTED**, not block on it.

## Current-state snapshot (for the Architect, §6 chaining)

- **Ingress ports in scope:** TCP 4317 (gRPC, `grpc_pass grpc://172.31.114.10:4317`), TCP 4318 (HTTP, `proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318`).
- **Current sender rule, both ports:** `allow all;` (unrestricted) — no `deny` line present in either block; first-match-wins semantics mean inserting any `allow <cidr>;` above the existing `allow all;` and changing `allow all;`→`deny all;` is sufficient and matches the file's own documented change procedure.
- **No other listeners on this surface to preserve/avoid** beyond the explicitly out-of-scope `operator-access.conf` (port 14318, different file, different purpose) — nothing else was returned by `get_ingress_config`.
- **Enforcement point confirmed singular:** ingress nginx is the only source-address control; `otel_collector` itself has no source-address control (per the ingress file's own header comment and the service descriptor).
- **Gap to carry forward:** true-source-IP visibility at the nginx enforcement point is **UNTESTED** — no tool on this read-only surface can confirm absence of an intervening NAT/LB between exporter and ingress.
- **No credentials/secrets encountered** in this harvest (nginx config has none to redact).

Confidence: 88 — Solid and fully evidenced for the structural branch (existing-grant, verified against the literal witnessed file content, ingress existence confirmed via stack_health), with one explicitly named, un-closeable gap (principal-unseen) that is a genuine tool-surface limitation rather than an assumption on my part.

